1
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:24,000
Meet Carl. His wife. & the Love of his Life.

2
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:42,000
Three Night Stand is a romance, comedy, drama film directed by Pat Kiely.

3
00:12:54,000 --> 00:13:00,000
A man's plans for a romantic weekend go awry when he learns that his ex-girlfriend, whom he
still secretly loves, manages the ski lodge where he and his wife are staying.

4
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:18,000
Carl, played by Sam Huntington.

5
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:36,000
Sue, played by Meaghan Rath.

6
00:25:48,000 --> 00:25:54,000
Robyn, played by Emmanuelle Chriqui.

7
00:30:06,000 --> 00:30:12,000
Stacey, played by Reagan Pasternak.

8
00:34:24,000 --> 00:34:30,000
Doug, played by Jonathan Cherry.

9
00:38:42,000 --> 00:38:48,000
Aaron Berg, played by Dan Beirne.

10
00:43:00,000 --> 00:43:06,000
Stand by Me Doraemon (Japanese: STAND BY ME ドラえもん, Hepburn: Sutando Bai Mī Doraemon) is a 2014 Japanese animated science
fiction comedy-drama film based on the Doraemon manga series and directed by Ryūichi Yagi and Takashi Yamazaki.

11
00:47:18,000 --> 00:47:24,000
It was released on 8 August 2014.

12
00:51:36,000 --> 00:51:42,000
It is the highest-grossing film of the Doraemon franchise.

13
00:55:54,000 --> 00:56:00,000
Entertainment premiered an English-dubbed version of the film at the
Tokyo International Film Festival on 24 October 2014.

14
01:00:12,000 --> 01:00:18,000
Stand by Me Doraemon was commercially successful in Japan.

15
01:04:30,000 --> 01:04:36,000
It was number one on the box office charts for five consecutive weeks and was the second highest-grossing
animated film of 2014 in Japan, with a box office total of $183.4 million, behind Disney's Frozen.

16
01:08:48,000 --> 01:08:54,000
In February 2015, the film won the Japan Academy Prize for
Animation of the Year at the 38th Japan Academy Prize.

17
01:13:06,000 --> 01:13:12,000
A sequel was released on 20 November 2020 in Japan.

18
01:17:24,000 --> 01:17:30,000
The film is primarily based on the first chapter of the manga "All the Way From the Future", the 1973 chapter "Mountain Rescue", the 1980 chapter "Goodbye Shizuka", the 1984 chapter "Imprinting
Shizuka", the 1998 short film "Doraemon Comes Back" and the 1999 short film "Doraemon: Nobita's Night Before a Wedding", though several other chapters are briefly brought up as well.

19
01:21:42,000 --> 01:21:48,000
The tone is lighthearted and emotional.
